  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nSurface high pressure extending across the mid-Missouri Valley and \npersistent dry easterly to southeasterly low-level flow continue to \nanchor a quiet, seasonably cool air mass across the area early this \nmorning. To the southwest, an upper-level disturbance and associated \nsurface troughing across Texas and the southern Plains remain well \nremoved from our forecast area, keeping deep moisture transport and \nprecipitation south and west of the Kansas border today.\n\nMid and high-level cloud cover streaming north of the southern \nPlains system will gradually thin and clear from north to south \nthrough the mid-morning and afternoon. With weak easterly to \nsoutheasterly surface winds around 5 to 10 mph and clearing sky, \nafternoon highs today will reach the lower to mid 50s. Under clear \nskies and calm to light southeasterly winds tonight, efficient \nradiational cooling will allow overnight lows to drop to near or just\nbelow freezing.\n\nA pattern transition begins Monday as upper-level ridging builds \neastward across the central CONUS and surface high pressure shifts \ninto the Ohio Valley. Light southerly return flow develops Monday \nmorning, increasing to 5 to 15 mph with gusts to 20 to 25 mph across \nnorth-central Kansas (including Republic, Cloud, and Ottawa \ncounties) by afternoon. Abundant sunshine and weak warm air \nadvection will push Monday highs into the mid to upper 50s, followed \nby milder overnight lows in the upper 30s to mid 40s Monday night.\n\nBy Tuesday and Wednesday, amplified upper-level troughing moving \ninto the western U.S. will induce stronger lee cyclogenesis across \nthe High Plains. The resulting tightened surface pressure gradient \nacross Kansas will generate breezy southerly winds across the entire \narea. Sustained south winds of 15 to 25 mph with gusts of 30 to 40 \nmph are expected Tuesday and Wednesday across north-central Kansas, with\ngusts of 25 to 30 mph extending east across the Flint Hills and \nnortheast Kansas. This persistent warm advection will lift daytime \nhighs into the lower 60s Tuesday, and into the mid 60s Wednesday, \nwhile overnight lows moderate into the mid to upper 40s. Despite the \ngusty winds, gradually recovering boundary-layer moisture should keep\nfire weather concerns below critical thresholds, though cured fine \nfuels will warrant monitoring across north-central Kansas where winds\nwill be strongest.\n\nForecast spread increases late in the week as the western U.S. \ntrough ejects across the Rockies and into the Plains between \nThursday and Saturday. Ahead of this system, Thursday remains dry \nand mild with highs in the low to mid 60s. As large-scale ascent and \ngulf moisture return overspread eastern Kansas ahead of an \napproaching cold front, rain chances return Thursday night (around 20 \npercent) and increase to 40 to 70 percent Friday into Friday night, \nlingering into Saturday (around 50 percent). Because guidance \nvaries on the amplification and forward speed of the ejecting wave,\nas well as whether a secondary wave tracks across the central \nPlains, confidence in exact frontal timing, rainfall totals, and \npotential post-frontal cooling by next weekend remain low.",
